---
import "../styles/bootstrap.css";
import "../styles/main.css";
---

<!--
the header is arranged with Bootstrap classes that apply Flexbox layouting to the
containing elements. Flexbox is a way to automatically position several elements
one after another.

in this particular case, the "d-md-flex" and "justify-content-between" classes in
<header> make it so the <p> tag is moved to the furthest left possible, and <nav>
goes on the right.

inside <nav>, the "d-flex justify-content-md-end gap-3" means it shoves all items
as furthest right as it can, with a medium-sized gap.

you don't need to learn about Flexbox for most page elements, but when you have to
arrange elements in a particular way for different viewports, like a header, a grid
of images, or a list of links, you'll probably want it.

https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/utilities/flex/
-->
<header
    class="fs-4 px-0 py-3 container d-md-flex align-items-center justify-content-between"
>
    <p class="m-0">coolsite.net</p>
    <!--
    we also make use of Bootstrap's breakpoint system to change the layout if the
    viewport looking at the page gets too small for comfort. this basically just
    means that some Bootstrap classes are selectively added or removed based on
    the viewport size.

    "justify-content-md-end" means it'll apply the "justify-content-end" class
    ONLY when the viewport is medium (md) or bigger.

    https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/layout/breakpoints/
    -->
    <nav class="d-flex justify-content-md-end gap-3">
        <a href="/">home</a>
        <a href="/blog">blog</a>
        <a href="https://crouton.net">crouton</a>
        <a href="https://nikki.quest">nikki's site</a>
    </nav>
</header>
